<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip I, Duke of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1346–1361</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Philip of Rouvres</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip of Rouvres was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 1349 and the last of its Capetian dukes. Succeeding his grandfather Odo IV as a small child, he also inherited the counties of Artois and Burgundy and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-iii-countess-of-flanders">Margaret of Flanders</a>. His death from plague in 1361, aged fifteen, ended the line; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II took the duchy and in 1363 granted it to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-i-duke-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Philip I, Duke of Burgundy" width="282" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis DESCHAMPS DE PAS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhilippeRouvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
